From 7d45806cbaafb714fe9fd09bcbaa300750fddcd1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 03:34:25 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] feat(interaction): retain native held block-use sessions

Bedrock 1.26.60.5 holds StartItemUseOn across repeated block uses and sends StopItemUseOn only on physical release or cancellation. Simulation-triggered repeats suppress ordinary block interaction; the retained start action preserves trapdoor facing and half across camera and item changes.

Transport the physical control lifetime from aware Java clients for direct and ViaProxy sessions. Keep first input and simulation repeats distinct, stop at the final result position, and preserve independent uses for ordinary Java clients. A controlled native relay probe without start/stop actions retains interaction suppression but loses the original facing, establishing that both boundaries matter.

Focused state tests cover repeated targets, duplicate held snapshots, cancellation before use, idempotent release, and ordinary client behavior. Spatial continuation and client prediction require matching add-on verification; private captures and proprietary resources stay outside the patch.

Match each local block-use result to its Java prediction sequence. Native
height-limit captures emit no start action until placement becomes valid;
failed repeats report failure and carry no inventory delta. Defer the
start action until predicted success, retain simulation triggers after
failed attempts, and omit unchanged creative inventory actions. Mismatched
sequence metadata cannot leak into a later use.

Native input also follows the first successful block use-on with a normal
use-in-air phase using the original held snapshot. Removing only this
phase in a controlled native relay loses retained facing. Mirror it for
aware held block use. Matching Survival captures use empty inventory-action
lists and consume blocks authoritatively, so remove guessed placement
slot deltas instead of submitting duplicate inventory predictions.
